export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  "102|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. There are those who say that if one sitting beside the one praying is weak, it is permitted. NB: See in Taz s.k. 4 what he challenged. His words are astonishing—for one can say the leniency for an elder and an ill person applies only to the ill person and the elder: if the one praying already came and stood there, the ill person and the elder need not stand since he is ill and elderly; but the one praying commits a prohibition by standing there to pray near him, and behold he can stand on another side—and therefore the prohibition is upon the one praying. And one can say this view explains per the explanation of Tosafot that Or Zarua brought above on \"with You, Hashem\"—therefore it is well said that if it were not within four amos she would not be standing to pray there, for there is no leniency because he is elderly; and Taz wrote this reasoning later regarding one who enters his boundary, and here he did not notice this—it requires analysis; understand.",
  "10|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "(Seif 7) Garments that are open, etc. NB: See in Magen Avraham [s.k. 11] what he wrote that even for a karmelit it is forbidden, for one does not wear a sudar ab initio; see there. And see what I wrote on this incidentally in the compositions of Taz on Yoreh De'ah, mahadura 27, which gained its place in Hilchos Taarovos siman 101, and in his compositions siman 333; see there, with Hashem's help, and examine closely:",
  "11|beur-hagra|6|ו": "(ו) Seif 6 — per the Rambam it is invalid. As stated in Gittin 23a, if so, etc.",
  "11|chokhmat-shlomo|11|_": "(Section 11) In the gloss — and it is good to measure the measure of the knot, etc. NB: I do not know why, for the mishnah is explicit that it is included as a garment in chapter 28 of Kelim [mishnah 7] regarding \"three by three\" that they said — except for the fringe (melal), for all the more so; and one authority said \"three aligned on three.\" If so, it is proven explicitly that it is included as a garment — so why should it not be counted among the thumb-knot (agudal)? Examine this well.",
  "11|yad-ephraim|14|_": "Seif 14 (in Shulchan Aruch there in Rama) in what Rema wrote \"this means thumbbreadths\" — apparently according to what Magen Avraham wrote above seif katan 5 that Tosafos were in doubt whether to measure at the wide place or the narrow place; if so, here one should measure at the narrow place stringently. I found in Sefer Machazik Berachah in the name of the Ari z\"l that these three fingerbreadths are amah, kammatz, and zeret, and he brought support for his words from the words of Rabbeinu Yeshaya of Trani; and in Sefer Shalmei Tzibbur he wrote that Rabbeinu Yeshaya's words are cited in the words of Shelah Gevurim. He wrote that they hold one should measure at the narrow place, and three fingerbreadths on the thumb at the narrow place is one measure together with the three fingerbreadths mentioned — see there at length.",
  "13|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "(Seif 3) If it became known to him on Shabbos, etc., that human dignity is great—see: the source of this law is from the Gemara chapter 3 of Berachos. I find it difficult on the Gemara: why did it not challenge from what is said in the chapter Kol Kitvei on 21a, that R. Yosi said, \"I never violated the words of my colleagues—if they told me to go up to the platform I would go up,\" and behold there is truly a positive-commandment prohibition as explained in chapter 2 of Kesubos; why was he permitted to violate a positive commandment because of his colleague's dignity? Perforce human dignity is great and pushes aside a Torah prohibition. Tosafos there in Kol Kitvei wondered the opposite—what prohibition is there in this ascent? See Magen Avraham [siman 128] what he wrote on this; but if there is a prohibition their wonder is resolved, and behold on the contrary if there is a prohibition how was he permitted to act because of human dignity? There it is a \"stand and act,\" and one must say that for a positive commandment it works well that human dignity pushes it aside, but for a negative commandment it does not push aside. From this there is proof for the view of those who hold a negative commandment is stricter than a positive one, and that which a positive commandment pushes aside a negative one is a scriptural decree; but for those who hold a positive commandment is stricter in the sugya of \"they feed him the lighter of the light\"—see there—the later authorities brought their dispute; it is very difficult from here, for if a positive commandment pushes aside human dignity, all the more so a negative commandment. It is also difficult: behold Tosafos wrote there that the prohibition is that it is a blessing in vain, and behold this is in the negative commandment of \"you shall not take [the Name in vain],\" and how was it permitted because of human dignity? For those who hold every manner of blessing is rabbinic it works well; but for those who hold even a manner of blessing is Torah law it is difficult why it did not challenge from here—and this requires analysis for now, investigate and understand.\n\nI was in doubt regarding the law that human dignity is great and pushes aside a Torah prohibition: if concern for disgrace continues for him through his negligence, does it push aside a Torah prohibition or not? At first glance it would seem to learn a fortiori from what we hold that a positive commandment pushes aside a negative one, yet nevertheless for a positive commandment here through negligence it does not push aside, as Tosafos wrote—and behold there is an a fortiori: a positive commandment pushes aside a negative one even a Torah negative commandment, yet through negligence it does not push aside; all the more so for human dignity, which does not push aside a Torah negative commandment but only a rabbinic one—all the more so through negligence it does not push aside.\n\nYet if so it is difficult: what does the Gemara challenge in Berachos (20a) in what Rav said—one who finds shaatnez in his garment removes it even in the marketplace—and it challenges from what Mar said that human dignity is great and pushes aside a negative commandment; what is the challenge? Say that there it deals with a case not through negligence, therefore it pushes aside; not so for one who finds shaatnez in his garment, which is through negligence, for he could have checked first whether it contains shaatnez—for regarding touching consecrated items we apply light to severe, and likewise in \"their valuations became mixed\" Tosafos considered it a negligent positive commandment, and all the more so here which is a negligent positive commandment—therefore it does not push aside; what is the challenge?\n\nIt is even more difficult from what is said in Eruvin [41b] where it says there: R. Nachman said—if one became insane he has only four cubits—and it challenges: what is he teaching us? It is obvious: now one whom gentiles took out has only four cubits; one who became insane from distress—what is the challenge? Behold afterward we say there that he asked Rav Huna: if he needed to relieve himself, what? He told them: human dignity is great and pushes aside a Torah negative commandment; if so, say Shmuel teaches us that one who became insane has only four cubits even if he needs to relieve himself—there is no need to say human dignity because it is through negligence of becoming insane; rather it is certainly proven that even through negligence we say human dignity is great. In truth it is difficult: what is the reason and whence does the Gemara know to say so and to challenge—perhaps through negligence we do not say human dignity is great? This requires analysis.\n\nBehold in what Shulchan Aruch wrote: if it became known to him on Shabbos while in a karmelis that the tallis on him is invalid, etc.—behold such is the view of the Mordechai, who proved thus from the Gemara of Menachos that he establishes it in a rabbinic karmelis, etc. At first glance there is no reason for the matter, for although from the side of Shabbos it is only a rabbinic prohibition, nevertheless there is a Torah prohibition here because he is wearing a four-cornered garment without tzitzis; from this the Mordechai proved there is no prohibition to wear a garment without tzitzis, only where it is in his power to make tzitzis is he obligated to do so; but where it is impossible for him, it is permitted to go thus—and R. Eliezer of Metz disagrees. To reconcile according to his view the Mordechai's difficulty, it appears R. Eliezer of Metz will explain per the Rambam's view that a doubtful case is lenient from the Torah and strict rabbinically. Behold the view of some decisors that there is a distinction between a doubt of karet or not—for in a doubt of karet the Rambam concedes the doubt is strict from the Torah. Therefore it works well: behold in Berachos the Gemara challenges per the one who says human dignity does not push aside a Torah prohibition, from a baraisa of one who was going to slaughter his Paschal offering, etc., and answers that Scripture revealed \"and for his sister,\" and challenges and we conclude from it, and answers \"sit and do not act is different,\" and Rashi challenged there: behold for an unattended corpse it is \"stand and act,\" and Rashi answered that there this prohibition is not stated regarding an unattended corpse—see there in Chiddushei Yeshanim and Penei Yehoshua what they wrote on the intent of Rashi's words.\n\nBehold in my work Sefer HaChaim [I wrote] that this will depend on the dispute whether impurity is set aside in the community or permitted—for per the one who says impurity was permitted in the community, one can say likewise for an unattended corpse; but if we say impurity is set aside in the community, behold it is also said \"in its place at its time\"—even in impurity there is no reason that an individual is set aside and the community is not set aside, yet nevertheless it is set aside; if so, likewise an unattended corpse is set aside, and if so one can again say we conclude from it that even for \"stand and act\" human dignity pushes aside a Torah prohibition.\n\nYet in Sefer HaChaim we wrote: behold in the Gemara it asks there whether burial is because of disgrace or because of atonement—and see in Tosafos who explained that the question is whether it is because of disgrace alone or disgrace together with atonement—for disgrace certainly exists too; if so the Gemara again does not challenge and we do not conclude from it that one must say it is different there because human dignity together with atonement pushes aside the prohibition, not so alone—only that we wrote since this question remains in the Gemara whether because of disgrace or atonement, therefore the Gemara challenges and we conclude from it and establish that burial is because of atonement, and therefore it pushed aside and did not establish, only that one must say \"sit and do not act is different\" and a nazir with an unattended corpse. In truth one can say he holds like the one who says impurity was permitted in the community, and always one can explain because of atonement—and that is specifically there; but in Menachos with Mar brei d'Ravina—for Ravina holds in Yoma 6b explicitly that impurity is set aside in the community, and if so Mar brei presumably holds like his father as we say many times in the Gemara—since he holds it is set aside, the Gemara's difficulty works well and we can conclude from it.\n\nYet one can reject: one can say there it is because of atonement together with disgrace, but human dignity alone does not push aside a Torah prohibition—yet behold this is an unresolved question and it is a doubt; therefore it works well that for Shabbos, which is court-imposed death, the doubt is strict from the Torah, and if so perhaps there with an unattended corpse it is because of atonement but human dignity does not push aside a Torah prohibition, and therefore he must answer with a rabbinic karmelis; but for the obligation of tzitzis behold it is only a positive commandment and the doubt is lenient from the Torah, and one can say burial is because of disgrace, and automatically it is proven that human dignity pushes aside a Torah prohibition even for \"stand and act\"—only rabbinically the doubt is strict, and again rabbinically human dignity is great; and the view of R. Eliezer of Metz who holds like the Rambam works well. But without human dignity one can certainly say there is a Torah prohibition to go without tzitzis, and not like the Mordechai—and the Mordechai can be said to hold like those who hold a Torah doubt is strict from the Torah, therefore he proves his law well as I explained here, investigate and understand; see what I wrote on this matter somewhat in my work on Yoreh Deah siman 372 regarding the law of priests' impurity in the aforementioned Rashi—see there and you will understand well, investigate and understand.\n\n(In that gloss) And even a tallis katan, etc., by force of human dignity. See in Magen Avraham s.k. 8 what my brother challenged, etc., and as is clear in Shabbos 131b where it says \"since it is in his power to declare them ownerless,\" etc.; see in Shulchan Aruch HaRav chapter 7 of Laws of Lulav in the name of Peri Chadash and Ritva explicitly to the contrary of his words that even to declare ownerless is forbidden on Shabbos—and see what I wrote on this in Orach Chayyim Laws of Shabbos siman 36 in my work Orach Chayyim from the year 5556 rejecting the words of Magen Avraham—see there, investigate and understand.\n\nBehold what is astonishing in the Rambam's words in Laws of Kilayim—what he wrote that they push aside the Paschal offering because of an unattended corpse, as it is written \"and for his sister,\" and he did not copy the reason that \"sit and do not act is different\"—see in my work Choshen Mishpat introduction \"Koneh Mekomo\" Laws of Presumption of Possession siman 146 in the sugya of \"this one says of my ancestors\" what I discussed there from topic to topic; I have still come to resolve the Rambam's words per the sugya there—see there with Hashem's help, investigate and understand.",
  "15|yad-ephraim|5|_": "(Seif 5 in Shulchan Aruch) At the time of tearing here one should note Taz note 5.",
  "1|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "(Section 5) It is good to say, etc., and the parashah of the burnt-offering, etc. NB: Regarding what everyone wondered about the Tur — that he placed olah before chatas — see his explanation, with Hashem's help, in his work Avodas Avodah on Avodah Zarah page 9, and in his work on the Torah, parashas Vayishlach (year 5601), on the verse \"for now I have seen your face as one sees the face of God,\" and in his work on the Torah, parashas Tzav, year [5546], and further in his novellae on the Torah — see there and understand.",
  "1|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "(Section 7) When he finishes the parashah of the olah, etc. NB: See Magen Avraham [note 10], what he wrote in the name of the Gaon Mahari z\"l that the nazir's asham comes for atonement. I wonder at him — how was it hidden from him the arranged Talmud in Shevuos [37a], which says there: From this we learn that a tamei nazir is exempt from lashes — behold, a korban is written regarding him there, that he brings a korban so that nezirus of taharah should take effect upon him; and Rashi explained \"behold, a korban is written regarding him\" — \"and he shall be a nazir to God the days of his nezirus, and he shall bring a lamb in its first year,\" etc. — behold, it challenges there from asham. And in any case, the baraisa there deals with asham, for it distinguishes there between nazir and shevuas hafkadan; and for an oath one is obligated asham, and nevertheless it answers there that it is not for atonement but only so that nezirus of taharah should take effect upon him, as Rashi wrote there, namely: so that nezirus of taharah should take effect upon him, that he begins counting from now, and Scripture ties it to the korban and it does not come for atonement — see there, explicitly the opposite of his words; it requires investigation; understand.",
  "20|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. One may not sell a tallit with tzitzit to a non-Jew, etc. See note: See in Magen Avraham what he wrote in the name of Rashi, that the Talmud's intent in what we say \"because of a prostitute\" is that she will say a Jew gave it to her. And behold, at first glance I have a difficulty: why did Rashi not explain that the concern is lest he mislead the prostitute into saying that he is a Jew—since we establish that one suspected regarding a light matter is not suspected regarding a matter more severe than it; and if so, it is known that adultery with a non-Jew is more severe than with a Jew regarding an unmarried woman; and all the more for those who hold that \"you shall not marry them\" applies to Gentile women—for certainly there is a prohibition more severe than Torah law; and also for those who hold \"you shall not marry them\" applies to converts—nevertheless in the eyes of the world it is certainly more severe with a non-Jew than with a Jew. And if so, behold one suspected lightly in the eyes of the world is not suspected severely in the eyes of the world; and if so, it is fitting to make a regulation to reduce prohibition—for with a Jew, if she is suspect, who among all Israel is suspected to listen to her to commit adultery with her? But a non-Jew is certainly suspected regarding adultery, and he will come to mislead her and she will think he is a Jew and commit adultery with him. And behold, I again looked in Tractate Menachos [page 43] and saw that he indeed explained thus—that Scripture without a verse, and he explained that she transgresses because of \"you shall not marry them,\" which proves he holds that \"you shall not marry them\" means Gentile women; and per his approach it follows, as Beit Shmuel wrote in his name in Even HaEzer [siman 4], and so is Rambam's view; and this is puzzling regarding the Magen Avraham who omitted Rashi's remark: And behold, through this I stood on Rambam's view, that he omitted this reason of the Talmud \"because of a prostitute\" and cited only R' Yehuda's reason \"lest he accompany him\"—the Beit Yosef sensed this and forced it because it is more common and more severe, but that is forced. But in my view it fits very well, for behold we have a Mishnah in chapter 3 of Demai [Mishnah 5]: One who gives tithes to an innkeeper—what he gives her, etc., R' Yosi says we are not responsible for Arameans, etc.; and it is explained in the Gemara Bava Kamma [page 69 side 2] the reason: feed him to the wicked and let him die; and so holds Rashbag in Tractate Maaser Sheni chapter 5 regarding the rest of the seven-year cycle: feed him to the wicked and let him die; and see Tosefes Yom Tov there that per Rashi in chapter 1 of Chullin, the Tanna Kamma also holds this view of feeding the wicked and letting him die—only that he holds an innkeeper intends for good; but from the Yerushalmi it is proven there that Tanna Kamma and R' Yosi dispute, for according to Tanna Kamma we do not say feed the wicked and let him die, and R' Yosi says we do—see there. Therefore Rambam holds like Rashi's first explanation, that the intent is \"because of a prostitute\"—that he will show her he is a Jew and she will commit adultery with him; and therefore it was difficult for me, for behold we are not responsible for Arameans; and therefore he holds this reason \"because of a prostitute\" holds like Tanna Kamma of Demai above, who holds we make regulations for Arameans, and therefore he holds the reason is \"because of a prostitute\"; and therefore from this reason itself R' Yehuda deduces another reason, \"lest he accompany him,\" for it was difficult for him: it works out for the Rabbis, but for R' Yosi who holds we are not responsible for Arameans, for him it would be forbidden to sell him a tallit; therefore R' Yehuda innovates another reason, \"lest he accompany him\"; and since we hold like Rashbag that we are not responsible for Arameans, therefore he could not copy the reason \"because of a prostitute,\" only the second reason. And what will sweeten this language of the Talmud in Menachos there, where it says: what is the reason here—translate it as \"because of a prostitute,\" and what is the language \"here translate it\"? But per the above it fits well, for behold Rashi's view is that even for Tanna Kamma we are not responsible for Arameans, and the reason there was from the fact that she intends for good—only in the Yerushalmi Tosefes Yom Tov brought that per Tanna Kamma we are not responsible for deceivers, and therefore he said \"here translate it\" in Eretz Yisrael, who holds that for Tanna Kamma we make regulations for Arameans, and therefore he explains \"because of a prostitute\"; but our Talmud holds like Rashi that even for Tanna Kamma we are not responsible for Arameans—if so, one cannot say the reason at all stems from a prostitute, and therefore R' Yehuda, who was in Babylonia, needed another reason, \"lest he accompany him,\" etc.; and it fits very well—understand well: And behold in our above words we can also reconcile what Peri Megadim critiqued in this siman on Rashi, that in adultery by chance there is certainly no Torah prohibition of \"you shall not marry them\"—see there; and per what I said, Rashi's intent would be thus: they made a regulation for this, lest when she knows he is a non-Jew she withdraws—and even with a non-Jew it is only rabbinic; and behold regarding rabbinic law she is suspect—in any case, in the eyes of the world adultery with a non-Jew is severe; and that is: in the eyes of the world it seems to them that \"you shall not marry them\" applies even by way of adultery, and as we say in general regarding \"you shall not covet a man's wife without money\"—it seems to them thus; so too are matters reversed—that it seems to them always Torah prohibitions even when not forbidden by Torah; and therefore here too, per the world's view, she would transgress \"you shall not marry them\" and would not have transgressed if she knew he was a non-Jew; but if she did not know he was a non-Jew, she would commit adultery with him; and since it is possible to withdraw from prohibition, we make a regulation—and it fits well. And per what was said, it also fits well what I wrote in my composition on Yoreh De'ah in the omissions, siman 118: if he is not wicked except regarding a rabbinic prohibition, we certainly make him a regulation so he should not come to Torah prohibition; and per the above explanation of Rashi, here there was Torah prohibition and the prostitute was unmarried—it was only rabbinic; if so, all agree it is fitting to make a regulation, and our words were concealed in this; but per the above it fits well that even with a non-Jew it was only rabbinic prohibition, only more severe in the eyes of the world; therefore it depends on the dispute of rabbinic authorities and R' Yosi; and Rambam's view fits well—understand well; and see in my Mahadura Tanina on Yoreh De'ah siman 118 what I wrote on these matters, and in my composition on Avodah Zarah page 44 in responsum to KK of Moshchisk in Avodah Zarah siman 109—see there and understand well.",
  "2|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "{Rama: Seif 6} — and he shall not walk four cubos. See Beit Yosef below siman 301 brought in Taz there s.k. 27.",
  "31|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. On Chol HaMoed it is likewise forbidden, etc. Footnote: see Magen Avraham s\"k 2 what he wrote — that one who dons on Chol HaMoed does not transgress bal tosif since he does not intend to add — meaning he does because of doubt whether perhaps Chol HaMoed is obligated in tefillin, etc. Behold at first glance this contradicts Mordekhai in first chapter of Megillah, who challenged there: why do we not read the megillah on the 15th also from the power of doubt of the day? And he answered there with one answer that one transgresses bal tosif, and it is proven that even in doubt bal tosif applies. However one may say that from this there is proof for the view of Radbaz that Magen Avraham brought [siman 690] that a rabbinic mitzvah does not require intent — and therefore it comes out well that the intent of Semak and Magen Avraham is: since he does not intend to add, and mitzvot require intent, therefore without intent he does not transgress bal tosif; but in a rabbinic mitzvah that does not require intent, even without intent he transgresses bal tosif. And even though in the Gemara Rosh Hashanah chapter 3 we conclude that even for fulfillment intent is not required, and to transgress not in its time certainly intent is not required — that is only if he does not intend at all for the mitzvah; nevertheless to be transgressive, bal tosif requires intent; but where he intends for the mitzvah only that we say since he does not intend for a complete mitzvah but only from doubt, there is no bal tosif — in this one may well distinguish: specifically if mitzvot require intent, then even in such a case he does not transgress bal tosif; but if mitzvot do not require intent, even for bal tosif such intent is not required — therefore rabbinic mitzvot are inferior to Torah mitzvot: for Torah mitzvot, since they require intent, he does not transgress bal tosif; but rabbinic, since they do not require intent, he transgresses bal tosif. However, since megillah is words of Kabbalah and like Torah law, therefore it requires intent — and again why should we be concerned for bal tosif? And perforce one must hold that even in doubt bal tosif is a concern. However one may say the intent of Mordekhai is thus: behold there he answered with another answer that one transgresses lo ya'avor; and one may say both are one: behold at first glance one may say that since it is only doubt whether he transgresses lo ya'avor, it is appropriate to call it rabbinic doubt and be lenient; and one should not say that conversely it is also rabbinic doubt and lenient — this is not so, for regarding obligation to read, since in any case he became obligated to read and it is doubt whether he is discharged, it is appropriate to call the doubt stringent; not so regarding lo ya'avor, where there is no certainty whether he transgressed lo ya'avor or not — if so it is appropriate to obligate him to read from doubt. But one may say megillah is words of Kabbalah and like Torah law — if so this lo ya'avor is also like Torah doubt and therefore the doubt is stringent, etc. Therefore it comes out well that Mordekchai was uncertain whether megillah is entirely words of Kabbalah and like Torah law or not — and therefore he resolves either way: if it is like Torah law and bal tosif from the power that intent is required and he does not transgress without intent, and behold he does only from doubt — but then the answer is from lo ya'avor, for Torah doubt is stringent; and if its law is not like Torah law and the doubt is lenient, and lo ya'avor is not relevant as above — then automatically, since it is rabbinic and does not require intent, bal tosif is relevant; and either way he resolves. However in truth without this our question stands from what Mordekhai wrote in his words that even when not in time bal tosif does not apply — nevertheless, since there are those established for today, etc. If so it comes out well: behold the Gemara concludes in Rosh Hashanah that to transgress in its time intent is not required, not in its time intent is required — if so, on Chol HaMoed, if it is not time for tefillin at all, then it is not in its time and intent is required, and behold he does not add but only from doubt as Magen Avraham wrote; but there on the 15th, since in any case it is their time for those established, therefore even without intent he transgresses bal tosif — and it comes out well; investigate.",
  "32|chokhmat-shlomo|25|_": "Se'if 25 — And likewise if there were not some yodin on the alephs, shins, and ayins, etc. See note: this apparently contradicts what Shulchan Aruch wrote above [se'if 1] in the Hagahah — \"however, if we see that the form of the letter does not remain as corrected, it is invalid even if a child reads it according to its law.\" If so, here too, if the yodin of the ayins and alephs do not touch, it is plain that it is not the form of a letter according to its law — so why would a child's reading help here? Therefore Taz wrote explicitly, in the name of Rivash: therefore one may not validate an aleph whose left leg does not touch the middle line just because a child calls it aleph, etc. — how did Shulchan Aruch write here the opposite? It is true one could say Shulchan Aruch's intent is specifically for correction it is permitted and it would not be out of order; but in any case, if they did not correct it, it is invalid, and a child's reading does not help except to qualify it to correct afterward and it would not be out of order — but whatever was not corrected is invalid. This is dealt with in se'if 1. But in truth there is no reason for the matter — either way, if through a child's reading it has the name and form of a letter, then without correction too it should be validated; and if it does not have the form of a letter on it, then again why should it not be considered out of order if he writes afterward, since until now it did not have the name of a letter — why should it not be considered out of order? Furthermore, if even though it has no letter form and is not considered out of order, why does it depend on whether the child reads it as a letter or not — since it has no letter form and is not considered out of order, why should there be a distinction whether the child recognizes it or not? Therefore we say the main distinction is whether their separation is evident — if their separation is evident, what the child reads does not help and it is invalid even if corrected afterward, and it is out of order; this is dealt with in se'if 1. Here it deals with a case where their separation is not evident — whatever the child calls it, it has the form of a letter and is not out of order; automatically, even if they did not correct it yet, it is also kosher. So teaches Shulchan Aruch's language itself above in se'if 1, which wrote \"likewise these protrusions of the zayins that do not touch and their separation is not evident,\" etc. — it implies this is his main reason: since their separation is not evident, the same applies to yodin on alephs and ayins, as is clear to me and correct. I again saw in Peri Megadim in Eshel Avraham that he wrote thus, and I mean his view in this; see there briefly, and understand well.",
  "32|rabbi-akiva-eiger|44|_": "Seif 44: the titura of the tefillin. And it appears that the batim and the ma'avarta and the titura are from one hide and people did not practice thus. But they make the titura and the ma'avarta from another hide that is not from the hide of the batim; and see Kol Bo chapter 23 of Hilchot Tefillin and Maharikash.",
  "32|rabbi-akiva-eiger|67|_": "Seif 67: and that one wrap hide. In Pri Dishna he disputes this. And in his view, if one made a four-bayit [shel rosh] intending to have a shel yad to wrap hide upon them, it does not help, since his making was not according to law. And specifically that case in seif 42 where the initial making was valid for shel rosh tefillin — wrapping hide in that case helps. Also there one may say they were permitted to wrap hide only for a time, because of pressing need when one has no shel yad tefillin; but to leave them thus wrapped in hide one may say it is forbidden.",
  "32|yad-ephraim|31|_": "(seif 31) That another reads it to him; and the same law applies if he writes by heart because it is fluent in his mouth — so too in Beit Yosef in the name of Ri:",
  "37|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3 in the gloss: and one day. I wonder — for this is by law obligated in tefillin, for regarding Torah law we are concerned lest the hairs fell out, as stated in Niddah 46a. And according to what Beit Chadash wrote in siman 255, that if he brought two hairs before thirteen and they are still on him and did not fall out, we are not concerned that he brought other hairs and they fell — it works well here to say that it deals with such a case.",
  "38|chokhmat-shlomo|8|_": "Seif 8—scribes of tefillin, etc. NB: see in my novellae to Mahadura Batra, siman 66, on the passage of those who swear to treasurers—what I sat there on the Rambam's words, that he omitted this baraita; see there at length that there is a distinction between a mitzvah that is necessary for him or not; see there and examine well.",
  "38|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5, obligated. But he should not put on tefillin of rabbinic origin all seven days—so write the writings of the Ari.",
  "3|chokhmat-shlomo|14|_": "(S. 14) “He shall take care not to hold his organ while urinating,” etc. Editor’s note: See Magen Avraham’s astonishment at Beit Yosef regarding “we are not expert in the measure of anxiety” unlike Niddah, etc.; see Chiddushei Mei Niddah 14, Chiddushei L’YD omissions EH 184 on resolving this, Chiddushei L’Shas 9, and Tosafos Niddah 14 s.v. ha’ochez—even where holding is permitted, when pressed it is forbidden—see there; poskim did not spell this out clearly. In this connection I saw in Machatzit HaShekel this siman: he challenged the Gemara’s proof from bolas—we say in Ketubot 27b that for intercourse there is “leisure” because the yetzer for arayos is very strong, so one might say the same here and the proof from libation is weak. In my humble opinion it is not difficult: there the case is gentiles and villains suspected of arayos—reasonably fear might leave their hearts and they would sin. Here, are we dealing with deliberate evildoers forming [forbidden] thoughts? Surely not—if he could intend that, he could sin even without urinating; we do not assume deliberate intent, only that while holding the organ involuntary thoughts may come and his heart is “compelled”; since he does not deliberately remove fear from his heart, as long as he remains afraid he will not come to [forbidden] thoughts by coercion while afraid—and a coerced thought here is no worse than coerced libation there; if there libation from his own mind even when “beloved” does not come from fear, so too a coerced unwanted thought will not arise while he is afraid—unlike Ketubot, where we deal with a deliberate sinner who at the moment of intercourse removes fear from his heart because of the yetzer for arayos that is “beloved” to him. Similarly Taz Yoreh De’ah 184: one must worry that at relations fear leaves the heart—see there; this fits; study it.",
  "3|rabbi-akiva-eiger|11|_": "{Rama: Seif 11} — Because of sorcery: three things are hard on sorcery—one who pulls spit from a fold [of garment], one who kills a louse on his garment, and one who wipes with earthenware. Shabbat 82a.",
  "3|rabbi-akiva-eiger|12|_": "{Rama: Seif 12} — One should relieve oneself modestly. Pesachim 112a: we say one who stands naked before a lamp will be stricken [with convulsions]. Ri Korkus copied this. In She’arei Chayyim (siman 620) he wrote: when one leaves the bathroom he should inspect the place he sat on so there is no filth—lest someone come suddenly, or at night, and sit on it. A person should not make one bathroom directly opposite another so that the privy opening is exposed toward the other bathroom or bathhouse.",
  "3|rabbi-akiva-eiger|17|_": "Seif 17 — One who holds back his openings for urination: one who holds back flatulence does not transgress bal teshaktzu. So too in Teshurat Hen (siman 16), and so too in Maharikash.",
  "40|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7 gloss until he wipes keri. I question: how do we challenge — shall we establish R' Yochanan in Bava Kamma forbids tefillin? One may say baal keri who wiped may don though not immersed — only concern lest he see keri while tefillin on him, as explained; here keri is on him — requires investigation; see responsa Basheir Rosh siman 20.",
  "42|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3 in the gloss: Parchment processed for tefillin — it is forbidden to write mundane words on it, for designation for the body of kedushah counts. NB: See in Magen Avraham, who wrote that this means the parchment of the titzah on the shin. And apparently it is difficult: if so, why did Shulchan Aruch write in seif 1 that if they were new and he had not yet donned them, it is permitted to make from head to hand — namely, because whatever he had not donned was only designation that does not count; yet in the titzah, designation counts? It resolves well: the main reason designation for the body of kedushah counts was because it requires processing leshemah; therefore designation counted. If so, it works out if one wishes to make mundane use of it — for since it was processed leshemah, he cannot make mundane use of it. But if he wishes to change from severe kedushah to minor kedushah, since he need not declare at the time of processing for the name of severe kedushah, again it is designation that does not count, since here he does not need processing for severe kedushah. Therefore Shulchan Aruch deals with changing from head to hand — for here he does not need intent for the head compartment; therefore it is permitted to change. But in the gloss it deals with actual mundane need — for here, since it requires processing leshemah, he cannot change to mundane use; and this resolves well. I later found the same in Magen Avraham itself above, siman 32, who wrote thus — and it pleases me. From this I learned the law in what I was asked: he placed head and hand parshiyot of Rashi inside Rashi batim, and afterward reconsidered to make them Rabbeinu Tam tefillin; I was asked whether it is permitted to change them, since from the outset he placed Rashi parshiyot there. I answered that apparently it seems permitted, for from what is written here, that if they were new and he had not donned them, it is permitted to make head into hand — which implies that even though he already placed the parshiyot there, only that he had not donned them, it is permitted to make head into hand; for this is considered only designation, and it is not similar to what we say about a pouch that he wrapped and designated, where it is forbidden to change — for there it is a finished act with no act after it, and therefore it is a [finished] act and forbidden to change. But here the main act is donning on the head; therefore placing parshiyot inside the batim is not a finished act — therefore it is only like designation that does not count; if so, here too it is permitted to change from Rashi to Rabbeinu Tam. But after reconsideration I say it is forbidden, since we proved from Rama’s words that for actual mundane need it is forbidden to change, since it requires processing leshemah. If so, for hand tefillin, which were certainly kedushah, only light kedushah — in this it is permitted to change, since he does not need processing for the severe. But for Rabbeinu Tam, since there is a dispute, and per poskim who hold like Rashi, Rabbeinu Tam tefillin are not tefillin at all and are to them like actual mundane matter — and for mundane matter it is forbidden to change — therefore for those poskim it was like actual mundane matter, and one must be concerned for stringency; therefore, since they were placed for Rashi, it was at least like designation, and designation for the body of kedushah counts, since the change would be for mundane use in their view; one must be concerned for stringency and it is forbidden to change. Perhaps even with intent alone to be Rashi tefillin it is forbidden to change to Rabbeinu Tam, as I explained — correct; examine.",
  "4|rabbi-akiva-eiger|18|_": "{Rama: Seif 18} And one who exits the bathroom — he took in his hand a cloth to wipe and saw he need not use the bathroom and did not relieve himself — even so he shall not touch sacred books until he washes his hands. Shulchan Aruch, siman 423. Brought in Eliyah Rabbah siman 7:",
  "4|yad-ephraim|7|_": "Seif 7 in all these matters — meaning, even regarding a vessel and human force [on the water] — it is proper to be scrupulous about this; unlike disqualified water which is more deficient, for one does not bless upon it, as above in seif 30. And regarding this Rama wrote \"but it does not hinder etc.\" — his intent is that even though the author wrote one should be scrupulous in all these matters, nevertheless this is only where it is possible; but if it is not possible it does not hinder — neither what is invalid because of the vessel nor because of human force. But regarding disqualified water of siman 164 one must be scrupulous even be'dieved when it is not possible, and one must go back after kosher [water], since some say one does not bless upon it:",
  "51|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "Se'if 7 in the gloss: \"And they say the verse 'Ve'anachnu nivarech,' etc.\" — Note: In these days the renowned rabbi, Moharan R' Shmuel, sent me his book Iyun Tefillah; and whenever I set my study upon it I saw that he picks a quarrel with the world's custom in pesukei dezimra — that they repeat \"Halleluyah\" and wait on the word \"hamotar\" for the chazan — and he wonders, for any verse that Moses did not pause at in the Torah, we do not pause at. I say, Heaven forbid to pick a quarrel with the custom of Israel; for besides that there are several customs that divide the verse, as the Magen Avraham wrote in several matters — and one may say that they hold that only in the Torah do we not pause, but in Nevi'im and Ketuvim we do pause; and similarly several poskim hold regarding matters written [in Scripture] that one may not recite them orally, for it is like Torah — so one may say here as well. And apart from this, it appears that one may say: since here they conclude with \"Halleluyah,\" therefore even though he returns and says \"Halleluyah,\" which is the beginning of the following verse — one may say that perhaps one repeats only the preceding word where the break is recognizable; then we do not pause. But where the break is not recognizable, we pause properly; only to repeat the word itself one is not permitted, for it is like one who says \"Shema Shema,\" and they silence him. But this depends on intent: if he truly repeats, it is forbidden, for it appears, Heaven forbid, like two authorities. But here, in truth, his intent is not to repeat but to begin from the psalm that follows; and so, in truth, there is no repetition here, and the verses are not considered interrupted, since one may say he is repeating the preceding word — and from where do we forbid in such a case? I found support for me in the Beit Yosef [siman 51] that some early authorities hold to repeat every verse at the end of each chapter in this manner — only some challenged this because it is like saying \"Shema Shema,\" etc. If so, it appears they did so intentionally to be a compromise between the views — to repeat only the last word, which is the beginning of the second chapter; this is not like \"Shema Shema\" and not like interrupting a verse in the middle [and one who examines the Beit Yosef, siman 51, will find what he wrote on the reason they say \"Ve'anachnu nivarech Yah\" — for otherwise one need not continue \"Halleluyah\" from the beginning of the following verse upward; the above will show his intent, as stated — that if he had not concluded with \"Halleluyah\" it would not be possible to continue upward, because one may not interrupt Scripture; but if they conclude with \"Halleluyah,\" one may continue upward for the reason stated]. And behold — for Israel, their custom is Torah; understand and examine. And if they are not prophets, they are sons of prophets; may my portion be with them in the World to Come, Amen.",
  "51|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4 — Between the psalms. What is permitted to interrupt in Keriat Shema as explained below section 66, all the more so here where it is permitted; and see in Eliyah Rabbah there who was more lenient, for here it is permitted to interrupt for all Amens and Modim derabbanan and the like.",
  "53|chokhmat-shlomo|15|_": "(seif 15) permanent shliach tzibur, etc., and he need not wait to be told, etc. NB: Here I saw in Shulchan Aruch of the pious Gaon of Vilna, who wrote that it is puzzling from what we say in Arakhin [page 11b] from the case of even though he consults on behalf of others; and in my humble opinion, after forgiveness from the rabbi, two separate matters: here the author's intention is thus—a different person who is not permanent should not be the one to begin the matter and ask to pray, for this is arrogance, as if he is fit to pray; therefore he must wait until others tell him first, and they are the ones who begin with him first. But a permanent shliach tzibur has no arrogance and he is permitted to begin the matter and go down before the ark; therefore he wrote that a permanent shliach tzibur goes down by himself and others need not tell him first. But nevertheless, even though he begins and goes down first, he must still consult with the congregation first—and this is what Arakhin deals with: he will be the one to begin and ask them whether he should pray. And this is clear and correct in my humble opinion, examine well.",
  "53|chokhmat-shlomo|19|_": "(seif 19) there are those who prevent, etc., and even an individual, etc. NB: see Beit Yosef—the reason is that it is like a sacrifice, and one may not offer a person's sacrifice against his will; and it was brought in Avkat Rochel. It is difficult for me—for on the contrary, from the words of Tosafot in Kiddushin [page 28b] regarding 'these kohanim are agents of the Merciful One' it is proven the opposite: from their words it is clear that if they were our agents he could object; implying that if they were agents of the Merciful One he cannot say he does not want that kohen and sacrifice against his will—and behold we hold kohanim are agents of the Merciful One; if so, it is proven the opposite. And this is: that shliach tzibur is different, for he is our agent. In any case, the language of Beit Yosef is not precise in its reason; rather, on the contrary, if it were similar to a sacrifice it would be appropriate that one not be able to object [and see what I wrote at length in the words of Tosafot, with Hashem's help, to the community of Lvov, in my omissions to tractate Nedarim page 35, still in manuscript, see there, examine well]: (ibid.) In the above seif, 'if not that they already agreed on him beforehand.' NB: see in my composition Mishpetei Tzedek, Choshen Mishpat part 2 siman 288, in my composition and in Shulchan Aruch siman 231, what was written in a responsum to the community of Kantshek—that therefore Shulchan Aruch was precise 'if not that they agreed on him beforehand' and did not write 'if he did not protest beforehand'—for one could say that through silence he can afterward appeal and say he erred in the law and thought an individual cannot protest, and now he knows the law that he can protest; only if he agreed explicitly he cannot afterward claim any argument. See there his reason, with Hashem's help, examine well.",
  "53|rabbi-akiva-eiger|25|_": "{Rama: who sings gentile songs.} In Kol Bo before us it states \"songs of the raven,\" and so I copied in responsum Torat Chaim part 3 siman 1. And he wrote above: we come to this because of a light matter in people's eyes — to sing songs of the raven — we say he should be removed, etc. End quote.",
  "55|chokhmat-shlomo|10|_": "(Seif 10.) If there is one youth, etc., and the thirteenth year is not intercalated, etc. See note: we have the text in Zevachim [daf 25 amud bet]: Rava said — it is taught: a lamb, unblemished, male, one year old, that it be unblemished and one year old at the time of slaughter, at acceptance, at carrying, at sprinkling — from where? Scripture teaches: \"it shall be\" — all its states shall be only unblemished and one year old, etc. And is there anything that at slaughter is one year old and at carrying and sprinkling is two years old? Rava said: this teaches that hours disqualify in consecrated animals — thus the Gemara's words; and see Rashi. At first glance it was difficult for me — what is the question? One finds it: he was born on Rosh Chodesh Nisan and the like, and his year did not end until the next Rosh Chodesh Nisan; and on the thirtieth of Adar witnesses had not yet come to sanctify the month, and they thought it would be intercalated, and his year had not yet ended until the morrow when it would be Rosh Chodesh — and at slaughter he was one year old, and afterward between slaughter and sprinkling witnesses came and the court sanctified the month, and the day had already become a second year. However, one may say that here we say the matter was revealed retroactively, that even at slaughter he was two years old; and there is a practical difference in such a case: a minor who recited Birkat HaMazon or prayed and discharged others who were eighteen, and he was still in the category of minor, and afterward the court sanctified the month — he becomes an adult retroactively and those eighteen were discharged through him, and he need not bless and pray again; and likewise if he ate forbidden fat then, he is flogged and brings a sacrifice retroactively. However, this is difficult for me only according to those who explain in the name of Yerushalmi on the verse \"I will call to Hashem, who is exalted over me\" — for behold, if she was three years and one day old and the year was intercalated, her virginity returns; and Rama brought them in Even HaEzer siman 20, Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 189, and Magen Avraham here. If so, we may also say the baraita's intent is thus: we require that he be one year old specifically at slaughter — but if at slaughter he was not one year old but two years old, only that afterward he became one year old, such as when at slaughter the year was not intercalated and he already had more than a year, and afterward the court intercalated the year and retroactively he became within his year — that does not help here, since at slaughter he was not one year old; and we require specifically that at slaughter he be in the status of one year old. And for this reason the verse teaches: specifically if after the entire offering he becomes one year old retroactively it does not help, since all his offerings were in the presumption of two years; but if in one of the services, such as carrying, acceptance, or sprinkling, he then becomes one year old, it helps to validate it — therefore it says regarding carrying, acceptance, sprinkling — from where? It does not help if in that service he becomes one year old; and we require specifically at slaughter — therefore it says as written \"it shall be\" — all its acts shall be in one category; but if in one act it was as two years old, it does not help. And why does Rava challenge? Therefore it seemed to prove from this that Yerushalmi does not say so except regarding virginity returning, and you have in it only its novelty; but for other laws, if at first he was established in the presumption of a [regular] year, even if afterward they intercalated the year, he does not return retroactively — and specifically if they knew from the start it would be intercalated, then his year did not begin until the second, because the main existence was in the second and the first was only supplementary. And in what Yerushalmi wrote in the first chapter of Megillah — the second is main and the first supplementary — but if at the time of Adar they still did not know it would be intercalated and they established him for the month of Adar, truly the years do not return retroactively; and there is a practical difference from this for Magen Avraham's ruling, who wondered why the author wrote \"and the thirteenth year is not intercalated\" — for even if it is intercalated, one born on the twenty-ninth of first Adar becomes bar mitzvah on the twenty-ninth of the second — see there. And in my humble opinion it seems not as his words: granted, when one was born in a non-intercalated year and at the time it becomes intercalated, he does not become [bar mitzvah] until second Adar — one may say the second is main and it is Adar, and he was born only in Adar, therefore he does not become bar mitzvah until second Adar. And conversely, if born in an intercalated year in first Adar and in the thirteenth year it is not intercalated, there is concern that the first is the main Adar, and then his year is not fulfilled until the month of Adar day for day. But if at the hour he was born it was intercalated, when he becomes thirteen years the year is intercalated — if so, either way: if the first is main, even now the first is main and his year was fulfilled in the first; and if the second is main, behold he was not born in its main part but in the supplementary part, and his thirteenth year was fulfilled in the supplementary part — either way he already became thirteen, and why should he wait further? Magen Avraham seems to base mainly on Yerushalmi \"I will call to Hashem who is exalted over me,\" that it is like a scriptural decree without reason — for regarding virginity returning, which reason does not suggest, we say so; therefore he holds we say so here too. But according to what I proved, we do not say so except there, and we do not derive from it generally; if so, here too in such a case where the thirteenth year is intercalated, he becomes bar mitzvah in the first — and this is correct; it requires study [I again saw in later authorities who brought in the name of Or Zarua and responsa Me'il Tzedakah that they also disagreed with Magen Avraham on this — my intent was their view, which I raised in the law]. And since I spoke in the Zevachim sugya above, I will mention what puzzled me in what Rava said — this teaches hours disqualify in consecrated animals: what relevance has the language of disqualification? On the contrary, per the matter the hours are validating in consecrated animals — for if we did not say that in consecrated animals we count hour by hour, then immediately at the day's start he becomes a second year, as Rashi's meaning there and Tosafos in Rosh Hashana and Niddah and Shach in Choshen Mishpat siman 35 and Magen Avraham here — and he would be disqualified from morning; but if we require hours, then all whose hour is not complete is one year old and fit for offering — if so, on the contrary they would be hours validating in consecrated animals. And it is true one finds disqualification too in those requiring two or three years, and then one finds disqualification through requiring hours; nevertheless, why should Rava mention a different matter not under discussion and leave the body of the law he speaks of? And since one finds validation too, he should have listed this side that hours validate in consecrated animals — for the force of permission is preferable everywhere; this too is dealt with in its place — uncertain; I have no business with this now to examine it; perhaps Hashem will grant me another time to explain more — it requires study.",
  "55|rabbi-akiva-eiger|15|_": "Se'if 15 — he combines them; and all the more so if the nine are inside or outside. And the shaliach tzibbur in the doorway can bring them out. And in that of se'if 17 which is different, since he is in a large courtyard removed from the congregation — and specifically a shaliach tzibbur upon whom they rely; but one standing at the doorway and nine inside or outside do not combine. Perishah.",
  "59|chatam-sofer|3|_": "Seif 3 in Hagahah Like the first reasoning. NB: The custom is to wait for the shaliach tzibur; and in any case also in all the verses one waits, such as \"Mi chamocha ba'elim\" and \"Hashem yimloch,\" for the reason written above in Magen Avraham siman 59. However, also at \"Atah Hu\" one waits, and I have not heard a reason for this. And it seems to me because Tur wrote below siman 61 to interrupt between \"mamlikhim\" and \"Atah Hu\" so it should not appear like mentioning the Name of the dead, heaven forbid; and one could say here there is greater scruple because of the heretics who worship the dead; therefore they make a break between \"mamlikhim\" and \"Atah Hu\":",
  "60|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4: Some say mitzvot do not require kavanah, and some say mitzvot require kavanah, etc.—NB: behold, even according to this view itself there is a dispute whether one who intends explicitly not to fulfill—whether in that case too he fulfills or not. And it now seems to me there is clear proof that if one need not have kavanah, even one who intends explicitly not to fulfill fulfills; and the practical difference is for a rabbinic mitzvah, for all agree one need not have kavanah—even one who intends explicitly not to fulfill fulfills. And the proof is from tractate Menachot [7b]: there Rabbi Yochanan said, etc., \"sacred vessels sanctify without awareness,\" etc.—and the difficulty is: what of it that they sanctify without awareness? Here it is possible that he intends explicitly not to sanctify so that afterward he can gather; and perforce it is proven that if we do not require awareness, even if he stipulated explicitly it also does not help, and perforce it sanctifies; so too if mitzvot require kavanah, even if he intends or says explicitly not to fulfill, he fulfills—and this is clear proof in my humble opinion, with Hashem's help, and examine well. Behold, what was difficult for the view of those who hold that if he intended to fulfill, all agree he does not fulfill—from tractate Arakhin and tractate Sukkah—see what I wrote to resolve this in my responsa Toldot Yaakov siman 112, Laws of Mezuzah, in responsa to the community of Vishnitz, end quote, and examine well. See in Magen Avraham s.k. 3 in the name of Radbaz that for a rabbinic mitzvah all agree one need not have kavanah—and I wondered at what Pri Megadim wrote: the Ran in Rosh Hashanah regarding one who hears from a casual actor does not fulfill—for even per the one who says one need not have kavanah, if he intended for a different mitzvah it is worse, for a species destroys it—see there. And one should examine in rabbinic mitzvot what the law is if in this case too, in rabbinic law, kavanah is required since he intended for a different mitzvah, or in rabbinic law even in such a case one need not have kavanah. And it now seems prima facie to bring proof for this from tractate Yoma [88a]: there Rav said, \"the prayer of Ne'ilah exempts that of Ma'ariv,\" and he challenged from the beraita: on Motza'ei Yom Kippur one prays the order of seven [blessings], etc.—see there. And the difficulty is: why does he challenge—let us say Rav deals with when he comes to consult first, then we instruct him to intend in the prayer of Ne'ilah to exempt even Ma'ariv, and then he is truly exempt; but the beraita deals with when he did not intend in Ne'ilah to exempt Ma'ariv—in that case it is not worse than if he prayed Ma'ariv and did not intend to fulfill thereby, for per the one who holds it is an obligation he does not fulfill—all the more so in Ne'ilah he is not exempt if he did not intend for the sake of Ma'ariv. And one could say the challenge is according to his view that he holds in Chullin regarding a niddah who was compelled and immersed that she is permitted, and he holds that mitzvot do not require kavanah—what of it, for here since he also prayed Ne'ilah, certainly he requires kavanah, as the Ran wrote above, and perforce the reason is: since prayer is rabbinic, in rabbinic law even if it is for a different mitzvah one need not have kavanah, and examine well. And one should not challenge that to resolve \"one who intends explicitly not to fulfill\" according to this explanation it is not so—for behold the beraita teaches us the law lechat'chila one does not do, and it said: in the morning one prays seven, etc.; in Ne'ilah one prays seven and confesses; in Ma'ariv one prays the order of seven—if so, if we require specifically that he intend not to fulfill Ma'ariv, it should have mentioned this too: in Ne'ilah one prays seven and intends not to fulfill Ma'ariv—and perforce since it did not give him this advice, perforce it deals with when he did not intend explicitly not to fulfill; but if even stam he does not fulfill, it is a fine difficulty, for since it did not advise him to intend to fulfill Ma'ariv, perforce it deals with stam, therefore he did not fulfill Ma'ariv; and Rav deals with when he wishes to intend to exempt also Ma'ariv, then Ne'ilah exempts—and perforce for a rabbinic mitzvah, even if he intended for another purpose, one need not have kavanah; examine well. And see in my composition on Orach Chayim Mishnat Hasidim siman 8 in responsa to the community of Podehayev what he wrote on this law, end quote, and examine well. Behold the view of Rabbeinu Yona: even per the one who says mitzvot do not require kavanah, nevertheless speech requires kavanah certainly—and perforce speech is not like action. And in my humble opinion one can prove from Rashi in tractate Menachot [2a]: \"but thought that is evident to all,\" etc., and he said: this thought regarding kodashim is verbal expression with the mouth, etc.—what did Rashi need with this, and it is not Rashi's way to be a posek but an explainer, and this matter is not relevant here specifically to sacrifices—he should have explained so. But it appears Rashi's intent is to resolve what was difficult for the rabbis: why do the rabbis disqualify—behold we hold that action removes from the domain of action and from the domain of thought; so too here, his actions prove about him, for he is for this minchah—why should it be disqualified because of his thought that he thinks the opposite; therefore Rashi explains that this thought is speech, and speech is literally like action, as we say \"movement of the lips is action\"; so this act of speech removes the act that proves the opposite. And one could say the reverse: the act that proves removes the act of speech—but in any case, since both are equal, what is the superiority of this over that—one could say the reverse too, that the act of speech removes immediately the act that proves; and since one can say thus and one can say thus, we go stringently again. And further: if they are equal, this cancels against that and thought in the heart remains and disqualifies as well; or even if stam remains it is invalid—for in general stam is valid in a sacrifice, and thought in the heart does not disqualify because there is at least an act of offering, and it is not an act that proves action; at least stam is for its sake and valid, and thought in the heart does not disqualify here. But here, since there is speech not for its sake, which is also like action, therefore the act cancels against the act and it is as if there was no act at all; therefore stam also disqualifies, and all the more thought in the heart disqualifies—such is Rashi's intent in my humble opinion. And from this it will be proven that Rashi holds speech is literally like action, not like Rabbeinu Yona; examine well. Behold it is explicit in the poskim that even for those who hold one need not have kavanah, nevertheless one who intends explicitly not to fulfill does not fulfill; and one should be in doubt whether it suffices in the heart—if in his heart he intended not to fulfill, does he also not fulfill, or not: only things of the heart are not things, and only if he says he intends not to fulfill does he not fulfill. And here is a simple resolution from what we learned in the Yerushalmi tractate Shabbat chapter Rabbi Eliezer on milah: \"Israel circumcises the Cuthean and the Cuthean does not circumcise Israel, because he intends for Mount Gerizim\"—the words of Rabbi Yudan; Rabbi Yosi said: and where do we find that milah requires kavanah—let him circumcise and go until his soul departs, end quote. And behold \"let him circumcise and go until his soul departs\" has no understanding, and Ketzot HaChoshen's explanation is very strained—what is this curse to me or another; but it appears the intent is: the view of Tosafot that a gentile stam acts, and what is stam is for its sake and valid for a gentile, and what is not stam not for its sake is invalid for a gentile. Accordingly this is Rabbi Yosi's intent: Rabbi Yudan holds that even stam is for its sake—we require kavanah for the sake of the mitzvah, and therefore he disqualifies a gentile lest they intend for Mount Gerizim; and even though it is only things of the heart, nevertheless it is not harmful but not effective, and it is like stam, and stam is also invalid. But Rabbi Yosi: what is stam for its sake is valid without kavanah; only when he intends not for its sake is it invalid—and behold here too things of the heart are not harmful, only if he says explicitly so; but in the heart, things of the heart are not things. And therefore Rabbi Yosi said: where do we find milah for its sake—for stam is for its sake, and this is that one should not hang this on his pleasure; is it that he will circumcise and go until the soul of the one circumcised departs—certainly he does not intend that his soul depart, and therefore he intends only to fix the person, and therefore it is stam for its sake, and therefore we do not require kavanah; again, even if he intends not for its sake, things of the heart are not things. And it is proven from this that where one need not have kavanah, even if he intends not for its sake it does not help to accept it—only if he says explicitly so, and not in the heart, as I wrote now; perhaps God will grant me in another time to explain more; examine very well.",
  "60|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4—and such is the halachah. See Pri Megadim in the petichah to the 18 berakhot of Shacharit. One could say that from siman 60 it follows that it is Torah law. And if he did it without kavanah, one could say he returns to do the mitzvah with kavanah out of doubt and does not bless.",
  "61|chokhmat-shlomo|9|_": "Seif 9—it is forbidden to say Shema Shema twice, etc. NB: Behold in Selichot for Monday they enacted to say \"and nevertheless we are to Him and our eyes to Him.\" And in my humble opinion this is not correct, for behold in the mishnah in Sukkah chapter 5 it states there that he would say \"we are to Him and to Him our eyes,\" and the Gemara challenges there on page 53—for one who says Shema Shema we silence him, and it answers that they would say \"we are to Him and our eyes to Him\" in hope, and Rashi explained since two matters are mentioned he mentions it two times and not one matter two times we have no concern. If so it implies that without this it is forbidden to say two times. And one should not say that in the mishnah it grouped them as one \"we are to Him and to Him our eyes\"—for this it is forbidden, but for us who separate with the word \"and our eyes\" we have no concern—this is not correct at all, for from Rashi's words it depends whether he mentions two matters; if so what difference if he says \"and to Him praise\" or at the end, and behold in the end one word \"to Him\" is said on \"we\" and one on \"and our eyes,\" and from this if this is considered two matters even when saying consecutively we have no concern, and if it is not considered two matters what difference if he separates with \"and our eyes\" first. And further—if the separation helps, why does the Talmud add length \"and our eyes to Him in hope\"—it should have answered briefly that it must be reversed \"we are to Him and our eyes to Him,\" and perforce that even this does not help if he does not explain the language of hope. If so for our nusach it is like saying Shema Shema and is not correct. Therefore it is fitting to correct to say the Name only once: \"and nevertheless we and our eyes to Him,\" as in my humble opinion is correct—examine well. Seif 13—after the first verse one must say the bedtime Shema, etc. NB: Tzelach in chapter 4 of Pesachim wanted to recommend for those accustomed to say the bedtime Shema in a loud voice that what the Talmud says Nemeira did not say Moshe means only to interrupt between them between the parashiyot—with this, since they did not say Moshe one should not interrupt between them in our own person and must say quietly; but not in the middle of the parashah it is permitted to say in a loud voice. And behold although his words seemed plausible, his words are not correct according to truth—for the custom on Yom Kippur is to say it in a loud voice in the middle of Keriat Shema. It is satisfactory if the Talmud's intent is on account of the body of the verse that it should not be said in a loud voice because it is great praise fit only for ministering angels—it works well for Yom Kippur when we are like ministering angels it is permitted to say it in a loud voice; but if the intent is only on account of interruption between parashiyot—what difference all days of the year, what difference Yom Kippur, and what relevance that we are like ministering angels to interrupt between parashiyot. And also what it says Nemeira said Jacob—where is it proven Jacob said thus between parashiyot. Therefore certainly the main point is as plain. And therefore according to our custom that on Yom Kippur in Keriat Shema we say in a loud voice, it is not fitting to say it at all in a loud voice on other days of the year even not in Keriat Shema—for otherwise there would be two voices contradicting each other—as in my humble opinion is correct in this—examine well.",
  "61|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "{On seif 5} He extends in alef. See below siman 128 in Magen Avraham 63.",
  "62|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Se'if 4 — if due to illness. In Perishah he wrote that it is reasonable that if before the time for Keriat Shema passed the constraint passed, he must recite, for thought is not like speech. I have a question: if after the time for Keriat Shema passed, what difference does it make that we say he fulfilled? For even if he did not fulfill, what would he do? What difference is there for practical law whether they erred or not? It is strained to say the difference is for four matters in which Keriat Shema also has compensation — see above, siman 58. In this, since he fulfilled through thought at the time of constraint, he does not need compensation — strained.",
  "70|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3. One who marries a virgin is exempt from Keriat Shema, etc. Marginal note: see Magen Avraham, who wrote that even if he wishes to be strict on himself he is not permitted, for it appears presumptuous; and he debated whether the halachah follows Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel in this; and see Hilchos Taanis and Hilchos Tisha B'Av (siman 554), where one can say it is melacha he lacks time for. I saw in Tosafos Pesachim s.v. Ravina achal b'nekiyasa, and Rabbenu Tam's language is that Ravina did not wish to take the title and make himself a Torah scholar, so conclude. From this it is proven that Ravina held the halachah follows Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel—not everyone who wishes to take the title may come and take it. Even so it is very puzzling, for it is explicit in Shas Pesachim chapter 4 that when the observer says it is melacha he lacks time for, we certainly are not concerned for presumption; and here who knows whether he has a loan in nekiyasa, and that he is strict because he is a Torah scholar—behold it is more lenient than melacha on Tisha B'Av, where we are not concerned for presumption in that case; how much more so here—and this requires much study now. One cannot say that Ravina held like the rabbis in Pesachim chapter 4 who hold that even on Tisha B'Av we are concerned for this presumption, for Shas there asks one set of rabbis on another and answers—there, since everyone did it and he did not do it, it appears presumptuous; granted for melacha on Tisha B'Av the majority of the world works and it is something noticeable and well known, therefore if he does not do it it appears presumptuous; but here, is the majority of the world eating in nekiyasa? Certainly not; and if they eat, is it recognizable whether he is in nekiyasa or not? Certainly there is no presumption concern here; and Keriat Shema, which the rabbis hold has no presumption concern in this—therefore for both the rabbis and Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel there it does not apply. Perhaps Ravina holds the other answer there that answers the opposite, and he held Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel is concerned for presumption in every matter, and therefore here too we are concerned for presumption—and requires study why Tosafos and the commentaries did not notice this; put your mind to it.",
  "74|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5: The member among them. But touching the pocket — we have no concern with it.",
  "76|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Section 8: And found it afterwards. And if it was found outside four amot but within as much as one can see — see siman 86, seif 2.",
  "8|chokhmat-shlomo|10|_": "(Seif 10) If he wears, etc. In my view: see in the Taz what he wrote to reconcile the Beit Yosef's question from a convert — that initially the person is not fit, as with immersion of a convert, etc. In truth, after Mahari Chagiz, this is not comparable: for a convert it is impossible in any way to be fit initially, therefore it is considered \"before performance\"; not so here, for it is possible he will not wear it until after he washes his hands and blesses immediately before performance — and understand. It is correct and proper that the Beit Yosef challenged well; examine carefully.",
  "8|chokhmat-shlomo|15|_": "(Seif 15) If his tallit fell, etc. In my view: if a little fell from it and he put it on, and afterwards a little more fell and he put it on, and so on until combined it would be considered that all fell — there is room to question whether it is considered that all fell and he must bless, or not. It seems simple from what is stated in Eruvin [24a]: \"He breached an amah and fenced an amah,\" etc., until \"you said to us: new aspects have come here; so too new aspects have come here,\" in his name in the sugya, and Rashi, and we rule thus below [siman 258], seif 2. If so, here too: certainly if he wore a tallit without a blessing he must bless as long as it is still on him, as written above in the Shulchan Aruch; if so here too, even if a little fell and he put it on — since combined it is as if all fell — it is considered new aspects have come here, and it is like wearing a tallit without a blessing and he must bless. So too per the Taz [siman 16] in a tallit split in two and sewn back — one part of the tzitzis is nullified in any case and he must make new tzitzis, Beit Yosef. If so, if a little was split and sewn, and again a little split and sewn until combined the whole is split and there is new sewing after making the tzitzis — new aspects have come here and he must make tzitzis anew on one side a second time. So too one may learn from this to what is written in Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh Deah [siman 296], one who vowed from this house and it fell and he rebuilt — it is permitted to dwell in it. And in Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer (siman 95) regarding a widow's residence — if it fell it departed from him; if so, it seems the same if he rebuilt a little anew and again a little — even though initially most stood and it was considered the first house, nevertheless if combined little by little most became new, new aspects have come here and it is permitted regarding the vow prohibition; and regarding a widow's residence the heirs are exempt from giving her this residence — so it seems to my humble opinion, clear and correct. From this one may learn for every similar case; it fits well; examine carefully.",
  "8|chokhmat-shlomo|16|_": "(Seif 16) He slept in his tallit, etc. In my view: if at the time of the blessing his intent was that the garment is not exempted until noon or the like — whether he is obligated to bless afterwards or not. See in the Magen Avraham, laws of Sukkah (siman 639, comment 17) that plainly he need not bless again; but in Iggeres Azriel I saw he wrote that in truth yes; therefore for practical law it is uncertain now; examine carefully.",
  "8|chokhmat-shlomo|9|_": "(Seif 9) Before he blesses he should look at the tzitzis strings, etc. In my view: the reason the decisors wrote is that even for concern of nullifying a positive commandment we do not concern ourselves — we rely on presumption. Nevertheless regarding concern of a blessing because of \"you shall not carry\" we are concerned. It seems this is only for those who hold that a blessing can involve \"you shall not carry\"; and since there is concern of a biblical prohibition, as with tzitzis and \"you shall not carry\" — it is severe — therefore regarding blessing we are concerned; and according to them women are forbidden to bless on a positive commandment they are exempt from. But for us, per the Rama [siman 589] that the custom is women bless on positive commandments they are exempt from — the reason is that we hold that no blessing involves a biblical prohibition; and if so, even a blessing in vain is only rabbinic; therefore regarding concern of a biblical prohibition of going without tzitzis we do not concern ourselves and rely on presumption — all the more so for concern of blessing which is only rabbinic we need not concern ourselves. Therefore for our custom that women bless on positive commandments they are exempt from, there is room to be lenient to bless without examining tzitzis; and there is support to be lenient for one who is busy and rushed and has no time for the matter, that he may wear and bless thus — so it seems to my humble opinion; examine carefully. See what the Taz divides in this law and the Magen Avraham disagrees; see in my work Totafos on Yoreh Deah 44, laws of slaughter, siman 18, in responsum to K.K. Manisteris — proof there against the Taz, in his name; examine carefully. See in the Magen Avraham [comment 11] and what the Noda B'Yehuda challenged in his words contradicting what he wrote in siman 337; see in my work in omissions to Sefer STaM, responsum to K.K. Tshudnov — reconciliation in his name; still in manuscript in my work on Avodah Zarah, in his name; examine carefully. See in the Magen Avraham, comment cited above, what he wrote in the name of the Bach that presumption depends on action, and what he challenged. See in my work Orach Chayyim, year 537, page 54, in my responsum to K.K. Nickelsburg — it found its place in laws of Shabbos, siman 328, what he wrote there to reconcile the Bach's words, with Hashem's help; examine carefully.",
  "90|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "(Seif 8) That there is another synagogue. See Magen Avraham siman 471 sec. 12.",
  "91|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: Considered covering. And likewise hairs called peruk (wig). Nevertheless because of \"fear of the place\" one should be careful. Possibly specifically regarding mentioning the Name; but regarding going four cubits with uncovered head, which is only piety as Magen Avraham wrote S.K. 3 — one may say that with a peruk covering it is permitted and there is no concern for \"fear of the place\" here. See Beit Yosef below siman 301, brought in Taz there S.K. 27.",
  "92|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7 in hagahah: to touch places. And if he touched, thought is permitted — Eshel Avraham. Below siman 168, seif 9.",
  "92|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9: at his will. And it appears from this that one should not smoke tobacco during study — see there.",
  "93|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3: One may not stand, etc., except out of a matter of the heart that is settled halacha, etc. Note: I saw in the responsa of Terumat HaDeshen (siman 41) that he wondered why in prayer the Sages forbade standing except out of settled halacha, yet we do not forbid likewise for the bathhouse that one should go only out of settled halacha. And what he wrote to answer: in the bathhouse, since there is no sanctity in it, he himself will not come to contemplate; and he brought proof from what is stated in the Gemara, Pesachim chapter 1, that we say he himself regrets [eating from the sacrifice] — is he eating from it? See there. And behold, in my humble opinion this proof is astonishing and is not comparable: eating is different, for there he regrets this very thing to burn it, and we are not concerned that he will do the opposite of his intention; but here, does one go to the bathhouse in order not to study? Certainly not — he goes there for pleasure, and not so that he would not reflect; on the contrary, if it were permitted there too he would reflect. And also, are we dealing with an ignoramus? Behold, a Torah scholar knows it is forbidden to reflect during prayer, and he turns away more from all his thoughts during prayer than when walking to the bathhouse. But in truth there is no beginning to this question: granted, in prayer, even if some reflection comes to him by force, nevertheless it is nullified from the prayer — and what help is there in this a forced claim; but in the bathhouse it is explicit in the Gemara Zevachim and in the Shulchan Aruch that due to force it is permitted — if so, necessarily when willing he certainly will not reflect; and if by force there is no prohibition in the matter. And further, in prayer, since he has no awareness that he is standing in prayer at all, therefore we are concerned he will come to reflect and forget that he is standing in prayer; but in the bathhouse, behold the bath is before him and he stands naked — if so, he has not forgotten he is in the bath; and if he knows he is in the bath he will certainly remove the reflection by Torah law and remove it from his heart. And this is sufficient, and it is plain — examine well.",
  "94|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9 — seated when he can; and likewise when praying while walking — so wrote Magen Avraham below siman 110 comment 8; in Noda B'Yehuda there he challenged this, for here specifically when praying seated — see there.",
  "9|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "(Seif 4) If he put, etc., some tzitzis, etc. NB: It seems the same applies—some tzitzis of wool and some of flax one should not put, for what difference is this or that? We require all of one kind, for a corner of its kind also exempts; nevertheless combination of wool or flax does not help—the same applies that some wool and some flax does not help. Especially for us, since we have no techeles—if so flax and wool do not exempt except through the force of corner matching corner; therefore we require all of one kind. So I ruled for practical law, and so in my humble opinion; I have no leisure to extend on this now—perhaps God will grant me another time to explain more, and examine well.",
};
